-- Adds the accepted-but-unpublished MSR-SLAM paper (no PDF or DOI yet), places it after the
-- published papers, moves the DJI paper after it, and replaces the ICHORA page details with an abstract, key points and figures.
-- The proofreading PDF is intentionally not published; figures live in the portfolio-media bucket.
update public.publications set sort_order = 4 where id = 2;
update public.publications set details = $p$<h2>Abstract</h2><p>Indoor 3D mapping in GPS-denied environments requires drift-resilient LiDAR–IMU fusion to ensure geometric consistency over extended trajectories. This work integrates a Velodyne VLP-16 3D LiDAR and a Pixhawk Cube Orange+ IMU on a TurtleBot Waffle Pi running ROS 2 Humble, and evaluates RKO-LIO, a LiDAR–inertial odometry method that avoids sensor-specific modeling via a physics-based kinematic motion model with adaptive scan-to-map regularization. Geometric accuracy is validated against roll-meter ground-truth measurements across five indoor test objects, achieving an RMSE of 0.026 m, MAE of 0.022 m, and mean error of 0.69% (range: 0.03% to -1.47%). A controlled simulation comparison replays an identical sensor bag through RKO-LIO, LIO-SAM, and FAST-LIO in three Gazebo indoor environments. FAST-LIO achieves the lowest trajectory error in all scenes; LIO-SAM is accurate in feature-rich environments but diverges catastrophically in the bookstore scene (APE 21.7 m, map destroyed); RKO-LIO produces bounded-error trajectories across every environment (APE ≤0.494 m) without any per-scene parameter tuning. These results demonstrate that worst-case robustness—not peak accuracy—is the decisive property for real GPS-denied indoor deployment.</p><h2>In short</h2><p>A small ground robot maps indoor spaces without GPS using a LiDAR and an IMU. The paper tests RKO-LIO, a method designed to keep working when the sensors are imperfect or poorly synchronised, first on the real robot and then against two popular alternatives in simulation.</p><h2>Main contributions</h2><ul><li><strong>A complete mapping system:</strong> Velodyne VLP-16 and Pixhawk Cube Orange+ on a TurtleBot Waffle Pi under ROS 2 Humble.</li><li><strong>Accuracy check on the real robot:</strong> point-cloud lengths compared with roll-meter measurements of five indoor objects.</li><li><strong>A fair simulation comparison:</strong> the same recorded sensor data replayed through RKO-LIO, LIO-SAM and FAST-LIO in three indoor worlds.</li><li><strong>A conclusion about robustness:</strong> in uncontrolled GPS-denied deployment, worst-case behaviour matters more than peak accuracy.</li></ul><h2>The robot</h2><p>The platform is a TurtleBot Waffle Pi with a custom SolidWorks and 3D-printed housing. A Velodyne VLP-16 sits on top, with the Pixhawk Cube Orange+ IMU inside and an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin running ROS 2 Humble and RKO-LIO. Tests ran in basement rooms, corridors and small tunnels at Fırat University.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/ichora/turtlebot-platform.png" alt="Customized TurtleBot Waffle Pi with annotated sensor stack" /><figcaption>The customized TurtleBot Waffle Pi: Velodyne VLP-16, Pixhawk Cube Orange+, NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin in its housing, and the OpenCR controller.</figcaption></figure><h2>Accuracy on the real robot</h2><p>Five indoor objects were measured with a roll meter and compared with the same lengths taken from the 3D map. The map matched within 1.47% on every object.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/ichora/object-length-validation.png" alt="Object-length validation setup with the point-cloud map and five measured objects" /><figcaption>Object-length validation: the point-cloud map with the five measured objects A to E, and the matching indoor photographs.</figcaption></figure><ul><li><strong>Error:</strong> RMSE 0.026 m, MAE 0.022 m, mean error 0.69%.</li><li><strong>Against earlier work:</strong> a previous LIO-SAM deployment on the same sensor pair reported an RMSE of 0.355 m, so this is a 13.7 times reduction.</li><li><strong>Synchronisation:</strong> the corrupted maps seen with LIO-SAM because of sensor timing errors did not appear with RKO-LIO.</li></ul><h2>Simulation comparison</h2><p>In Gazebo, the same recorded data from a furnished house, a warehouse and a dense bookstore was replayed offline through each method, so the sensor input was identical.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/ichora/simulation-maps.png" alt="Reconstructed maps for each environment and method" /><figcaption>Reconstructed maps per environment and method. In the bookstore, the LIO-SAM map is destroyed.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/ichora/simulation-trajectories.png" alt="Estimated trajectories against ground truth in the three simulated environments" /><figcaption>Estimated trajectories against ground truth. In the bookstore, LIO-SAM spirals off track while RKO-LIO and FAST-LIO stay aligned.</figcaption></figure><ul><li><strong>FAST-LIO</strong> had the lowest error in every environment and is the better choice for known, feature-rich spaces with a high-rate IMU.</li><li><strong>LIO-SAM</strong> was accurate in easy scenes but diverged in the bookstore (APE 21.689 m) and lost the map.</li><li><strong>RKO-LIO</strong> was less accurate in easy scenes but stayed bounded everywhere (APE at most 0.494 m) without per-scene tuning.</li></ul><h2>Read the full paper</h2><p>The complete method, tables, figures and references are in the paper. Use the <strong>Open PDF</strong> button on this page, or the IEEE record linked here.</p><p>Funded by TÜBİTAK (grant 123E406) and the Fırat University Scientific Research Projects Unit (FUBAP). Part of the study was carried out within the master's thesis of Munef El Muhammed.</p>$p$ where doi = '10.1109/ICHORA69329.2026.11537209';
insert into public.publications (title, venue, publication_date, status, authors, summary, details, keywords, sort_order)
select 'MSR-SLAM: A Multi-Stage Map-Refinement Framework for Reliable LiDAR–Inertial SLAM in GPS-Denied Environments', 'Applied Sciences (MDPI), 2026', '2026', 'Accepted · Journal Article (in press)', 'Munef El Muhammed; Anas Mahyoub Naji Saeed Alqadhi; Mohammed Ali Mohammed S. Bajhaw; Aysegul Ucar',
  'A map-refinement back end for LiDAR–inertial SLAM that decides which loop closures deserve to change the map, using geometric verification and an online admission check, tested on a Unitree Go2 quadruped and KITTI.', $p$<h2>Abstract</h2><p>LiDAR–inertial odometry can remain locally accurate while accumulating unbounded global drift, and in structurally repetitive GPS-denied environments, the loop closures intended to remove that drift may instead corrupt the map. This paper presents MSR-SLAM, a multi-stage map-refinement framework built on an existing LiDAR–inertial odometry front-end. Loop candidates retrieved by a learned place-recognition descriptor are verified geometrically. In the offline batch configuration, geometrically verified loop constraints are incorporated into pose graph optimization after the complete sequence is available. In the online configuration, geometrically verified candidates pass through an additional admission stage that rejects constraints whose candidate-induced translational or rotational correction exceeds preset limits before they can affect the active pose graph. The global map is subsequently reconstructed from the optimized poses. The main real-robot evaluation comprised seven offline/online processing cases derived from five distinct recorded traversals in indoor, outdoor, and subterranean environments, using a Unitree Go2 quadruped carrying a 16-beam LiDAR. Additional live traversals were used separately for the online consistency analysis. In one online sequence, 14 of the 15 geometrically verified candidates were rejected at the admission stage; in a loop-free tunnel, all 310 retrieved candidates were rejected during geometric verification, and the trajectory was left unmodified. The offline failure case E4 combined excellent registration quality and large residual reduction with severe global distortion and no loop edge spanning 500 or more node indices, highlighting the need to assess loop-edge coverage alongside local fit. Three independent traversals of one environment produced geometric acceptance rates between 10.8% and 11.2%, with exactly one loop constraint admitted in each traversal. On KITTI Sequence 00, MSR-SLAM reduced the RKO-LIO ATE RMSE from 9.5525 m to 4.9322 m (48.4%) while leaving the short-term RPE essentially unchanged.</p><h2>Featured application</h2><p>The framework supports post-disaster and subterranean survey missions in which a legged robot must build a globally consistent three-dimensional map without satellite positioning, and in which an incorrect map correction is more damaging than an uncorrected one.</p><h2>In short</h2><p>A robot that maps with LiDAR slowly drifts, and loop closures are meant to fix that drift. In corridors, basements and tunnels, where places look alike, a wrong loop closure can ruin the map instead. MSR-SLAM treats the hard problem as deciding which loop closures deserve to change the map: candidates are retrieved by a learned descriptor, verified geometrically, and, when running online, a further check blocks any constraint that would force an implausibly large correction.</p><h2>Main contributions</h2><ul><li><strong>Multi-stage framework:</strong> place recognition, geometric verification, pose graph optimization and map reconstruction, run offline in batch or online in ROS 2 with motion-gated keyframes.</li><li><strong>Online admission control:</strong> each verified loop candidate is checked for the correction it would cause, and rejected above 0.7 m or 15 degrees before it can touch the active graph.</li><li><strong>A warning about local metrics:</strong> registration quality and residual reduction alone do not show that a refinement is correct.</li><li><strong>Negative-control evidence:</strong> a loop-free tunnel where nothing was admitted, and three independent traversals of one environment.</li></ul><h2>How it works</h2><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/framework-overview.png" alt="Overview of the MSR-SLAM framework" /><figcaption>Framework overview: the RKO-LIO front-end feeds a refinement back-end shared by the offline batch and online ROS 2 configurations. Grey blocks are adopted from prior work.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/admission-cascade.png" alt="Online three-stage filtering cascade and candidate attrition" /><figcaption>The online filtering cascade (left) and how candidates were whittled down in three online experiments (right).</figcaption></figure><h2>Real-robot experiments</h2><p>The system was tested on a Unitree Go2 quadruped carrying a 16-beam Hesai PandarXT-16 LiDAR and the robot's built-in IMU. Five recorded traversals, processed as seven offline and online cases, covered corridors, a basement, an outdoor building side and a subterranean tunnel.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/unitree-go2-platform.png" alt="Unitree Go2 quadruped with a Hesai PandarXT-16 LiDAR" /><figcaption>The Unitree Go2 quadruped with the 16-beam LiDAR.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/test-environments.png" alt="Representative views of the four test environments" /><figcaption>Test environments: (a) indoor corridor, (b) basement, (c) outdoor building side, (d) subterranean tunnel.</figcaption></figure><h2>Key findings</h2><ul><li><strong>An offline failure, reported openly:</strong> in sequence E4 the best registration scores and the largest residual reduction came with the worst global distortion, because no loop edge spanned 500 or more node indices. Local fit alone can hide a failing map.</li><li><strong>Admission control at work:</strong> online, 14 of 15 geometrically verified candidates in one sequence were stopped before reaching the graph. Without admission control, 14 edges were admitted instead of one and the maximum correction rose from 0.283 m to 1.235 m.</li><li><strong>Negative control:</strong> in a loop-free tunnel all 310 retrieved candidates were rejected by geometric verification and the trajectory was left unchanged.</li><li><strong>Repeatability:</strong> three independent traversals gave geometric acceptance rates of 10.8% to 11.2%, with exactly one loop constraint admitted each time.</li><li><strong>Physical check:</strong> four distances measured with a laser meter in one environment improved from 0.331 m to 0.031 m mean absolute error, a limited check dominated by one measurement.</li></ul><h2>Benchmark on KITTI</h2><p>On KITTI Sequence 00, MSR-SLAM reduced the RKO-LIO absolute trajectory error from 9.5525 m to 4.9322 m (48.4%) and the SE(3)-aligned error from 3.6584 m to 1.9395 m, while the short-term relative error stayed essentially unchanged. The benefit grows with segment length, which fits a method that suppresses accumulated drift rather than changing local odometry. The baseline methods were run with loop closure disabled, so this measures the gain from adding the back-end to RKO-LIO.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/kitti-trajectories.png" alt="Estimated trajectories on KITTI Sequence 00 compared with the reference" /><figcaption>KITTI Sequence 00: estimated trajectories against the reference. The MSR-SLAM trajectory stays closest to it over long distances.</figcaption></figure><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The robot experiments have no external ground truth, so absolute error is not reported. They use one platform and one LiDAR model, the place-recognition network was trained on 64-beam data, only three traversals were repeated, and the correctness of individual rejections was not independently verified.</p><h2>Full paper</h2><p>This paper has been accepted in Applied Sciences (MDPI) and is not yet published. The PDF and DOI will be added here once it appears online.</p><p>Funded by TÜBİTAK (grant 123E406) and the Fırat University Scientific Research Projects Unit (FÜBAP, grants MF.24.80, MF.25.154 and MF.25.155). The study was conducted as part of the master's thesis of Munef El Muhammed.</p>$p$, array['LiDAR–Inertial SLAM','Loop Closure','Place Recognition','Pose Graph Optimization','Map Refinement','Quadruped Robot','GPS-Denied Environments','Geometric Verification'], 3
where not exists (select 1 from public.publications where title = 'MSR-SLAM: A Multi-Stage Map-Refinement Framework for Reliable LiDAR–Inertial SLAM in GPS-Denied Environments');
